(i) $a > b$ and $a > c$;
(ii) $2R \geq r_{b}$ and $2R \geq r_{c}$.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer and define $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ by subtracting a nonzero digit of $x_{n-1}$ from $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer and define $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ by adding a nonzero digit of $x_{n-1}$ to $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains a number divisible by 3;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many numbers divisible by 3.

Let $x_{1}$ be a positive integer and define $x_{n}$ for $n \geq 2$ by adding a digit of $x_{n-1}$ (possibly zero) to $x_{n-1}$. Prove that
(a) the sequence contains an even number;
(b) the sequence contains infinitely many even numbers.

