Problem:

Sia $ABCD$ un rettangolo con $A B - B C > 0$ e sia $\omega$ la sua circonferenza circoscritta. Siano $E$ e $F$ rispettivamente le intersezioni (distinte da $A$) della bisettrice dell'angolo $\widehat{BAD}$ con il lato $CD$ e la circonferenza $\omega$. La perpendicolare a $DF$ passante per $E$ interseca la corda $DF$ in $G$ e l'arco $DF$ non contenente $C$ nel punto $H$. Si dimostri che:
(a) i segmenti $DF$ e $FB$ hanno la stessa lunghezza;
(b) i triangoli $DEG$ e $DHG$ sono congruenti;
(c) i segmenti $HF$ e $FC$ sono uguali.

negatives.text:
Sia $ABCD$ un rettangolo con $AB > BC$ e $\omega$ la sua circonferenza circoscritta. La bisettrice dell'angolo $\angle BAD$ interseca $CD$ in $E$ e $\omega$ in $F$ (diverso da $A$). La retta per $E$ parallela a $DF$ interseca $DF$ in $G$ e l'arco $DF$ non contenente $C$ in $H$. Provare che:
(a) $DF = FB$;
(b) $\triangle DEG$ e $\triangle DHG$ sono congruenti;
(c) $HF = FC$.

Sia $ABCD$ un rettangolo con $AB > BC$ e $\omega$ la sua circonferenza circoscritta. La bisettrice dell'angolo $\angle BAD$ interseca $CD$ in $E$ e $\omega$ in $F$ (diverso da $A$). La perpendicolare a $DF$ per $E$ interseca $DF$ in $G$ e l'arco $DF$ non contenente $C$ in $H$. Provare che:
(a) $DF = FC$;
(b) $\triangle DEG$ e $\triangle DHG$ sono congruenti;
(c) $HF = FC$.

Sia $ABCD$ un rettangolo con $AB < BC$ e $\omega$ la sua circonferenza circoscritta. La bisettrice dell'angolo $\angle BAD$ interseca $CD$ in $E$ e $\omega$ in $F$ (diverso da $A$). La perpendicolare a $DF$ per $E$ interseca $DF$ in $G$ e l'arco $DF$ non contenente $C$ in $H$. Provare che:
(a) $DF = FB$;
(b) $\triangle DEG$ e $\triangle DHG$ sono congruenti;
(c) $HF = FC$.

\text{Ambra costruisce una sequenza di numeri, a partire da un numero reale positivo } x_{0}, \text{come segue: } x_{n+1} = \left\{\frac{1}{x_{n}}\right\}, \text{dove } \{\alpha\} \text{rappresenta la parte frazionaria di } \alpha. \text{La sequenza termina quando } x_{n} = 0.

a. \text{Dimostrare che se } x_{0} = \frac{p}{q}, \text{con } p, q \in \mathbb{N}^{+}, \text{allora la sequenza termina.}

b. \text{Dimostrare che se la sequenza termina, allora } x_{0} \text{è razionale.}

\text{Ambra costruisce una sequenza di numeri, a partire da un numero reale positivo } x_{0}, \text{come segue: } x_{n+1} = \left\{1 + \frac{1}{x_{n}}\right\}, \text{dove } \{\alpha\} \text{rappresenta la parte frazionaria di } \alpha. \text{La sequenza termina quando } x_{n} = 0.

a. \text{Dimostrare che se } x_{0} = \frac{p}{q}, \text{con } p, q \in \mathbb{N}^{+}, \text{allora la sequenza termina.}

b. \text{Dimostrare che se la sequenza termina, allora } x_{0} \text{è razionale.}

\text{Ambra costruisce una sequenza di numeri, a partire da un numero reale positivo } x_{0}, \text{come segue: } x_{n+1} = \left\{1 - \frac{1}{x_{n}}\right\}, \text{dove } \{\alpha\} \text{rappresenta la parte frazionaria di } \alpha. \text{La sequenza termina quando } x_{n} = 1.

a. \text{Dimostrare che se } x_{0} = \frac{p}{q}, \text{con } p, q \in \mathbb{N}^{+}, \text{allora la sequenza termina.}

b. \text{Dimostrare che se la sequenza termina, allora } x_{0} \text{è razionale.}

\text{A group of } n > 1 \text{ pirates of different ages owned a total of 2009 coins. Initially, each pirate (except the youngest one) had two coins more than the next younger.}

\text{a) Find all possible values of } n.

\text{b) Every day a pirate was chosen. The chosen pirate gave a coin to each of the other pirates. If } n = 7, \text{ find the largest possible number of coins a pirate can have after several days.}

\text{A group of } n > 1 \text{ pirates of different ages owned a total of 2009 coins. Initially, each pirate (except the youngest one) had one coin more than the next younger.}

\text{a) Find all possible values of } n.

\text{b) Every day a pirate was chosen. The chosen pirate gave a coin to each of the other pirates. If } n = 6, \text{ find the largest possible number of coins a pirate can have after several days.}

In a group of $n$ people, each one had a different ball. They performed a sequence of swaps; in each swap, two people swapped the ball they had at that moment. Each pair of people performed at most one swap. In the end each person had the ball he/she had at the start. Find the least possible number of swaps, if:
a) $n=5$;
b) $n=6$.

In a group of $n$ people, each one had a different ball. They performed a sequence of swaps; in each swap, two people swapped the ball they had at that moment. Each pair of people performed at least one swap. In the end each person had a different ball from the one they had at the start. Find the least possible number of swaps, if:
a) $n=5$;
b) $n=6$.

In a group of $n$ people, each one had a different ball. They performed a sequence of swaps; in each swap, two people swapped the ball they had at that moment. Each pair of people performed at least one swap. In the end each person had the ball they had at the start, but the number of swaps must be even. Find the least possible number of swaps, if:
a) $n=5$;
b) $n=6$.

Find all positive integers $n$ such that $\frac{2^{4n+2} - 1}{65}$ is

a) an integer;

b) a prime.

Find all positive integers $n$ such that $\frac{2^{4n+2} + 1}{64}$ is

a) an integer;

b) a prime.

Find all positive integers $n$ such that $\frac{2^{4n+1} + 1}{65}$ is

a) an integer;

b) a prime.

Find the largest integer $k$ ($k \geq 2$) such that there exists an integer $n$ ($n \geq k$) where for every sequence of $n$ consecutive positive integers, there exist $k$ numbers among them such that:
1. none of the $k$ numbers is divisible by $6$, $7$, or $8$;
2. for any two distinct numbers in the $k$, their difference is divisible by all of $6$, $7$, and $8$.

Find the largest integer $k$ ($k \geq 2$) such that there exists an integer $n$ ($n \geq k$) where for every sequence of $n$ consecutive positive integers, there exist $k$ numbers among them such that:
1. none of the $k$ numbers is divisible by $6$, $7$, or $8$;
2. for any two distinct numbers in the $k$, their difference is not divisible by $6$ or $7$.

Let us call a point in the $xy$-plane a good point if each of its coordinates is an integer from $1$ to $2000$. A polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-shaped polyline if it satisfies:
* $x_1 < x_2$, $y_1 = y_2$;
* $x_2 < x_3$, $y_2 - x_2 = y_3 - x_3$;
* $x_3 < x_4$, $y_3 = y_4$.
Determine the smallest possible positive integer $n$ such that $n$ Z-shaped polylines cover all good points.

Let us call a point in the $xy$-plane a good point if each of its coordinates is an integer from $1$ to $2000$. A polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-shaped polyline if it satisfies:
* $x_1 < x_2$, $y_1 = y_2$;
* $x_2 > x_3$, $y_2 + x_2 = y_3 + x_3$;
* $x_3 < x_4$, $y_3 = y_4$.
Determine the smallest possible positive integer $n$ such that $n$ Z-shaped polylines cover all good points.

Let us call a point in the $xy$-plane a good point if each of its coordinates is an integer from $1$ to $2000$. A polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-shaped polyline if it satisfies:
* $x_1 < x_2$, $y_1 = y_2$;
* $x_2 > x_3$, $y_2 - x_2 = y_3 - x_3$;
* $x_3 < x_4$, $y_3 = y_4 + 1$.
Determine the smallest possible positive integer $n$ such that $n$ Z-shaped polylines cover all good points.

Three strictly increasing sequences of positive integers $a_n$, $b_n$, and $c_n$ partition the positive integers. For every $n$:
(i) $c_{a_n} = b_n - 1$;
(ii) $a_{n+1} > b_n$;
(iii) $c_n c_{n+1} - (n+1) c_{n+1} - n c_n$ is even.
Find $a_{2010}, b_{2010}, c_{2010}$.

Three strictly increasing sequences of positive integers $a_n$, $b_n$, and $c_n$ partition the positive integers. For every $n$:
(i) $c_{a_n} = b_n + 1$;
(ii) $a_{n+1} < b_n$;
(iii) $c_n c_{n+1} - (n+1) c_{n+1} - n c_n$ is even.
Find $a_{2010}, b_{2010}, c_{2010}$.

Three strictly increasing sequences of positive integers $a_n$, $b_n$, and $c_n$ partition the positive integers. For every $n$:
(i) $c_{a_n} = b_n + 1$;
(ii) $a_{n+1} > b_n$;
(iii) $c_n c_{n+1} - (n+1) c_{n+1} - n c_n$ is divisible by 4.
Find $a_{2010}, b_{2010}, c_{2010}$.

Find all surjective functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ such that for all $a, b \in \mathbb{N}$, exactly one of the following is true:
$$
\begin{aligned}
f(a) &= f(b) \\
f(a+b) &= \max \{f(a), f(b)\}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find all surjective functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ such that for all $a, b \in \mathbb{N}$, both of the following are true:
$$
\begin{aligned}
f(a) &= f(b) \\
f(a+b) &= \min \{f(a), f(b)\}
\end{aligned}
$$

Find all surjective functions $f: \mathbb{N} \rightarrow \mathbb{N}$ such that for all $a, b \in \mathbb{N}$, exactly one of the following is true:
$$
\begin{aligned}
f(a) &= f(b) \\
f(a-b) &= \min \{f(a), f(b)\}
\end{aligned}
$$

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all integers $s, t$, and $r$, if $s + \sqrt{2} t + \sqrt{3} r = 1$, then prove that $s = t = r = 0$.
\item For all integers $a, b, c$, and $d$, if the ratio $\frac{a + \sqrt{2} b + \sqrt{3} c}{b + \sqrt{2} c + \sqrt{3} d}$ is a rational number, then prove that $ad = bc + 1$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all integers $s, t$, and $r$, if $s + \sqrt{2} t + \sqrt{3} r = 0$, then prove that $s + t + r = 0$.
\item For all integers $a, b, c$, and $d$, if the ratio $\frac{a + \sqrt{2} b + \sqrt{3} c}{b + \sqrt{2} c + \sqrt{3} d}$ is a rational number, then prove that $a + d = b + c$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all integers $s, t$, and $r$, if $s + \sqrt{2} t + \sqrt{3} r = 0$, then prove that $s^2 + t^2 + r^2 = 0$.
\item For all integers $a, b, c$, and $d$, if the ratio $\frac{a + \sqrt{2} b + \sqrt{3} c}{b + \sqrt{2} c + \sqrt{3} d}$ is a rational number, then prove that $a^2 d = b^2 c$.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Problem:

Vind alle drietallen $(a, b, c)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a + c = 10 - b$ zodat er $a$ rode, $b$ blauwe en $c$ groene punten (allemaal verschillend) in het vlak bestaan met de volgende eigenschappen:
- voor elk rood punt en elk blauw punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $37$;
- voor elk groen punt en elk rood punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $30$;
- voor elk blauw punt en elk groen punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $1$;

negatives.text:
Vind alle drietallen $(a, b, c)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a+b+c=10$ zodat er $a$ rode, $b$ blauwe en $c$ groene punten (alle verschillend) in het vlak bestaan met de volgende eigenschappen:
- voor elk rood punt en elk blauw punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $-37$;
- voor elk groen punt en elk rood punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $30$;
- voor elk blauw punt en elk groen punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $1$;

Vind alle drietallen $(a, b, c)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a+b+c=10$ zodat er $a$ rode, $b$ blauwe en $c$ groene punten (alle verschillend) in het vlak bestaan met de volgende eigenschappen:
- voor elk rood punt en elk blauw punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; het product van al deze afstanden is $37$;
- voor elk groen punt en elk rood punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $30$;
- voor elk blauw punt en elk groen punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $1$;

Vind alle drietallen $(a, b, c)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a+b+c=10$ zodat er $a$ rode, $b$ blauwe en $c$ groene punten (alle verschillend) in het vlak bestaan met de volgende eigenschappen:
- voor elk rood punt en elk blauw punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $37$;
- voor elk groen punt en elk rood punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; de som van al deze afstanden is $30$;
- voor elk blauw punt en elk groen punt bekijken we de afstand tussen deze twee punten; het product van al deze afstanden is $1$;

positive_text:
Problem:

Een viertal $(a, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $a \leq b \leq c \leq d$ noemen we goed indien we ieder geheel getal rood, blauw, groen of paars kunnen kleuren zodat
- van iedere $a$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één rood is;
- van iedere $b$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één blauw is;
- van iedere $c$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één groen is;
- van iedere $d$ opeenvolgende getallen er tenminste één paars is.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen met $a - 2 = 0$.

negatives.text:
Een viertal $(2, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $2 \leq b \leq c \leq d$ is goed als er een kleurtoewijzing bestaat waarbij elk geheel getal gekleurd is met rood, blauw, groen of paars, zodat
- in iedere $2$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één rood is;
- in iedere $b$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één blauw is;
- in iedere $c$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één groen is;
- in iedere $d$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één paars is.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen.

Let $x_{11}, x_{21}, \ldots, x_{n1}$, $n>2$, be a sequence of integers with all $x_{i1}$ distinct. Define:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& x_{i, k+1} = x_{ik} + x_{i+1, k}, \quad i = 1, 2, \ldots, n-1 \\
& x_{n, k+1} = x_{nk} + x_{1k}
\end{aligned}
$$
Show that for $n$ odd, $x_{jk}$ is not an integer for some $j, k$. Does the same conclusion hold for $n$ even?

positive_text:
For a finite non-empty set of primes $W$, let $|W|$ be the size of the set $W$, and $m(W)$ be the largest possible number of consecutive positive integers, each of which is divisible by at least one member of $W$.
(i) Show that $|W| \le m(W)$, with equality if and only if $\min(W) > |W|$;
(ii) Show that $m(W) < (|W| + 1)(2^{|W|} - 1)$.

negatives.text:
Let $P$ be a finite non-empty set of primes. Define $|P|$ as the number of primes in $P$, and $m(P)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers, each divisible by at least one prime in $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \ge m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) > |P$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} + 1)$.

Let $P$ be a finite non-empty set of primes. Define $|P|$ as the number of primes in $P$, and $m(P)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers, each divisible by at least one prime in $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\max(P) > |P$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

positive_text:
Consider an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals. The parallel from the intersection point of the diagonals meets the non-parallel sides $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$ respectively. Point $Q$ is the mirror image of $P$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Show that

a) $- A D + Q R = 0$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

negatives.text:
In an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals, a line through the intersection point of the diagonals and parallel to the bases intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at $P$ and $R$, respectively. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ across the midpoint of $[AD]$. Prove that:

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

In an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals, a line through the intersection point of the diagonals and parallel to the bases intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at $P$ and $R$, respectively. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Prove that:

a) $QR = AB$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Show that $m^2 + m + 1$ is an element of the set $\{n^2 + n + 1 \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, for any positive integer $m$.
\item b) Let $p$ be a perfect square, $p > 1$. Prove that there exist positive integers $r$ and $q$ such that $p^2 + p + 1 = (r^2 + r - 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Show that $m^2 - m - 1$ is an element of the set $\{n^2 + n + 1 \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, for any positive integer $m$.
\item b) Let $p$ be a perfect square, $p > 1$. Prove that there exist positive integers $r$ and $q$ such that $p^2 + p + 1 = (r^2 - r + 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Show that $m^2 - m + 1$ is an element of the set $\{n^2 - n + 1 \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, for any positive integer $m$.
\item b) Let $p$ be a perfect square, $p > 1$. Prove that there exist positive integers $r$ and $q$ such that $p^2 - p + 1 = (r^2 + r + 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Find all the functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ fulfilling the conditions:
i) $f(u+w) = f(u)+f(w)+2mn$, for every integers $u, w$;
ii) $f(f(x))-f(1)$ is a perfect square for each integer $x$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) + f(1)$ is a perfect square for each integer $x$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for each natural number $x$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect cube for each integer $x$.

positive_text:
Problem:

a) Demonstraţi că, oricare ar fi numărul real $x$, are loc inegalitatea
$$- x^{3} - x + 1 \geq - x^{4}$$

b) Rezolvaţi în mulţimea numerelor reale sistemul
$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}=3 \\
x_{1}^{3}+x_{2}^{3}+x_{3}^{3}=x_{1}^{4}+x_{2}^{4}+x_{3}^{4}
\end{array}
\right.
$$

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(w) - f(u) < |w - u|$, for every $w, u \in I$;

ii) $\int_w^u f(t)dt - f(w)(u-w) \le \frac{1}{2}(w-u)^2$, for every $w, u \in I$.

negatives.text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) < \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x-y)$, for every $x, y \in I$.

\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is called *p-periodic* if } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is a repeating decimal with a period of length } p, \text{ and the cycle does not begin immediately after the decimal point. For example, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Find all p-periodic numbers } n \text{ so that the first digit of the period of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is not zero.}
\item Find the largest 4-periodic prime.
\end{enumerate}

\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is called *p-periodic* if } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is a repeating decimal with a period of length } p, \text{ and the cycle starts immediately after the decimal point. For instance, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Find all p-periodic numbers } n \text{ so that the first digit of the period of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is not zero.}
\item Find the smallest 4-periodic prime.
\end{enumerate}

\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is called *p-periodic* if } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is a repeating decimal with a period of length } p, \text{ and the cycle starts immediately after the decimal point. For example, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Find all p-periodic numbers } n \text{ so that the last digit of the period of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is not zero.}
\item Find the largest 4-periodic prime.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence of positive real numbers, $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $2 y_{f(n)} \geq y_{n}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers, converging to $0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence of real numbers $(y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, converging to $0$, such that $x_n \le y_n \le 2y_{f(n)}$, for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

negatives.text:
\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. We color each square of an } n \times n \text{ board using one of } n \text{ colors.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that, for any such coloring, there exists a path of a chess knight from the bottom-left to the upper-right corner that uses all the colors.
\item Prove that, if we reduce the number of colors to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 2, \text{ then the statement from a) is true for all } n.
\end{enumerate}

\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. We color each square of an } n \times n \text{ board using one of } n \text{ colors.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that, for any such coloring, there exists a path of a chess knight from the bottom-left to the upper-right corner that does not use all the colors.
\item Prove that, if we reduce the number of colors to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 1, \text{ then the statement from a) is true for all } n.
\end{enumerate}

\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. We color each square of an } n \times n \text{ board using one of } n \text{ colors.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that, for any such coloring, there exists a path of a chess knight from the bottom-left to the upper-right corner that uses at most } n - 2 \text{ colors.}
\item Prove that, if we reduce the number of colors to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 2, \text{ then the statement from a) is true for all } n.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
a) For every elements $a$ and $b$ from $A$ there exists two odd integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^{2} = - b^{2} + \left(a p + b q\right)^{2}$;
b) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
1) For every $a, b \in A$, there exist two even integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^2 + b^2 = (pa + qb)^2$;
2) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 0$.

Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
1) For every $a, b \in A$, there exist integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^2 - b^2 = (pa + qb)^2$;
2) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 0$.

Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
1) For every $a, b \in A$, there exist two odd integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^2 + b^2 = (pa + qb)^2$;
2) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 1$.

positive_text:
Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ for which:
i) $f'(w) = 0$, for all $w \in \mathbb{Z}$;
ii) if $f'(w) = 0$ for some $w \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(w) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
(2) if $f(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
(2) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 1$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$;
(2) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 0$.

positive_text:
Consider a triangle $ABC$, such that $\angle B = 90^\circ$. Denote by $I$ the in-center and let $F$, $D$ and $E$ be the points where the incircle touches sides $[AB]$, $[BC]$, and $[AC]$ respectively. If $CI \cap EF = \{M\}$ and $DM \cap AB = \{N\}$, show that:

a) $AI = ND$;

b) $F M - \frac{E I M}{C} = 0$.

negatives.text:
Să se determine funcţiile derivabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care verifică:

i) $f'(x)=0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;

ii) pentru $x \in \mathbb{R}$, dacă $f'(x)=0$, atunci $f(x)=1$.

Să se determine funcţiile derivabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care verifică:

i) $f'(x)=0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;

ii) pentru $x \in \mathbb{R}$, dacă $f(x)=0$, atunci $f'(x)=0$.

Să se determine funcţiile derivabile $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ care verifică:

i) $f'(x)=0$, pentru orice $x \in \mathbb{Q}$;

ii) pentru $x \in \mathbb{R}$, dacă $f'(x)=0$, atunci $f(x)=0$.

positive_text:
If $A$ and $B$ are positive integers, then $\overline{AB}$ will denote the number obtained by writing, in order, the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. For instance, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{A} \overline{B} - 1932016 = 0$.
Prove that there are infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{AB}$ in each of the following situations:
a) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;
b) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;
c) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;
d) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

negatives.text:
Let $A$ and $B$ be positive integers and $\overline{AB}$ be the number formed by writing the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect cubes of the form $\overline{AB}$ in the following situations:

i) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;

ii) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;

iii) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;

iv) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

positive_text:
The continuous functions $f, g: [0, \infty) \to [0, \infty)$ have the properties:
(i) $f(0) = g(0) = 0$;
(ii) $g(x) \neq 0$, for every $0 < x$;
(iii) $f(x + g(f(x))) = f(x)$, for every $x \ge 0$.
Show that $f(x) = 0$, for every $x \ge 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function such that $f((1 - t)x + ty) \ge \min\{f(x), f(y)\}$, for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and let $m = \sup_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Show that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-decreasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx \ge 0$.

Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function satisfying $f((1 - t)x + ty) \le \max\{f(x), f(y)\}$ for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and let $m = \inf_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Show that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-increasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx \ge 0$.

Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function such that $f((1 - t)x + ty) \le \max\{f(x), f(y)\}$ for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and let $m = \inf_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Show that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-decreasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx < 0$.

positive_text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n - n_{0} > 0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t)^2 \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = -1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

positive_text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and let $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$, $n \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Prove that:

a) if $p - 5 \geq 0$ is a prime number, then $p$ divides $S_p$;

b) $S_5$ is not a square.

negatives.text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$. Prove:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p$ divides $S_{p-1}$;
b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$. Prove:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p^2$ divides $S_p$;
b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$. Prove:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p$ divides $S_p$;
b) $S_5$ is a square.

positive_text:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
(i) $f(f(u^2) + w + f(w)) = u^2 + 2f(w)$;
(ii) $u \le w$ implies $f(u) \le f(w)$;
for all real numbers $u$ and $w$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 - 2f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + f(y)^2$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$.

Arătaţi că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăţi:
a) $m < n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc cel mult patru cifre distincte și $m$ este pătrat perfect.

Arătaţi că, pentru orice număr natural $n \geqslant 2$, există un multiplu $m$ al său, nenul, cu următoarele proprietăţi:
a) $m < n^{4}$;
b) în scrierea lui $m$ în baza 10 se folosesc exact patru cifre distincte.

positive_text:
Consider $A, W \in M_n(\mathbb{R})$, and the function $f: M_n(\mathbb{C}) \to M_n(\mathbb{C})$, defined by $f(U) = AZ + W\bar{U}$, $U \in M_n(\mathbb{C})$, where $\bar{U}$ is the matrix having as entries the conjugates of the entries of $U$. Prove that the following are equivalent:
(1) $f$ is injective;
(2) $f$ is surjective;
(3) matrices $A+W$ and $A-W$ are non-singular.

Find all the real numbers $x$ and $y$ so that:
(i) $x - 2 y^{2} \geq 0$;
(ii) $y \ge 2x^2$;
(iii) the number $8(x - y)$ is an integer.

We will call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, different from each other, with $b < a$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$. For instance, $225$ is cool because $225 = 15^2$ and $225 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

negatives.text:
We call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, distinct from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

We define a positive integer $n$ as cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, all distinct, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$ with $a < b$ and $c < d$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

A positive integer $n$ is cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, distinct from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 + d^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

positive_text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group, and $G > H$ be a proper subgroup of $G$. If there are endomorphisms $f, g, h : G \to G$ of the group $G$, such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ holds for any $x, y \in G \setminus H$, show that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian, and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

negatives.text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group with a proper subgroup $H < G$. Suppose $f, g, h$ are endomorphisms of $G$ such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ for all $x, y$ in $G$ with $x \in H$ and $y \notin H$. Prove that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

positive_text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a - b \leq 0$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

negatives.text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d - 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3544$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3542$.

positive_text:
Suppose $A, W \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ are such that $A + W = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(W)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB - BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is even, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) = \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

positive_text:
The total number of languages used in KAUST is $n$. For each positive integer $k \leq n$, let $A_{k}$ be the set of all those people in KAUST who can speak at least $k$ languages; and let $B_{k}$ be the set of all people $P$ in KAUST with the property that, for any $k$ pairwise different languages (used in KAUST), $P$ can speak at least one of these $k$ languages. Prove that

a. If $2 k - 1 \geq n$ then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$ then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

negatives.text:
Let $n$ be the number of languages at KAUST. For $1 \leq k \leq n$, define $A_{k}$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ languages, and $B_{k}$ as the set of individuals who can speak all $k$ distinct languages. Prove:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

positive_text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(w+1) \geq f(w)+1$ for all $w \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(w y) \geq f(w) f(y)$ for all $w, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \leq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \geq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \leq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x^2) \geq f(x)^2$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

Да ли постоји $n \times n$ таблица у којој су уписана сва броја од $1$ до $n^2$, таква да за сваку селекцију $n$ елемената, по један из сваке врсте и колоне, збир тих елемената има исти остатак при дељењу са $n^2 + 1$. Питање:

а. Да ли ово важи за $n = 8$;

б. Да ли ово важи за $n = 10$?

