Für reelle Zahlen $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
a=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+a}}, & b=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+b}} \\
c=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+c}}, & d=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+d}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass $a b c d = 2004$ gilt.

Für reelle Zahlen $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
a=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21-a}}, & b=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21-b}} \\
c=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+c}}, & d=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+d}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass $a + b + c + d = 2004$ gilt.

Prove that there is a positive real number $ c > 0 $ such that there are infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers satisfying:
(a) $ N < c \cdot 2^k n \log(n) $;
(b) $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) $ for every prime power $ p^e $ dividing $ N $.

Prove that there is a positive real number $ c > 0 $ such that there are infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers satisfying:
(a) $ N > c \cdot 2^k n \log(n) $;
(b) $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) + 1 $ for every prime power $ p^e $ dividing $ N $.

positive_text:
找出所有符合下列三個條件且定義在正整數上的非負整數值函數 $f$:
(1) 存在至少一個 $n$，有 $f(n) \neq 0$;
(2) 對於所有正整數 $x$ 和 $y$，有 $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$;
(3) 有無窮多個正整數 $n$ 對於所有 $n > k$ 有 $f(k) = f(n-k)$。

Determine all functions $f$ defined on the set of all positive integers and taking non-negative integer values, satisfying the three conditions:
(1) $f(n) \neq 0$ for at least one $n$;
(2) $f(xy) = f(x) + f(y)$ for every positive integers $x$ and $y$;
(3) there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ such that $f(k) = f(n-k)$ for all $k < n$.

There are $w$ cities in a country. A monopoly company intends to establish air traffic between some of these cities. The government demands that the air traffic satisfies the following conditions. People should be able to get from each city to any other of these cities by one or several flights. Besides there must be an equal number of flights from each city. Note that two flights — from $A$ to $B$ and from $B$ to $A$ — are considered as one flight between cities $A$ and $B$. Moreover, there may be at most one flight between two cities. But the company wants to cancel as little flights as possible so that you can not get from each city to any other city. What minimal number of flights has the company to cancel if:
a) $w = 2008$;
b) $w = 2007$?

negatives.text:
There are $n$ cities in a country. A monopoly company establishes air traffic between some of these cities such that the graph is connected and each city has at most two flights. Flights are undirected. The company wants to remove as few flights as possible so that the graph becomes disconnected. Determine the minimal number of flights to remove for:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2007$.

positive_text:
Let's designate through $P(w)$ the product of digits of the integer non-negative number $w$. Prove that sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:

a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(w)}{P(w^2)} \right\}$, where $w$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $w^2$ does not contain zero in the decimal record;

b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(w^2)}{P(w)} \right\}$, where $w$ belongs to the set of such whole non-negative numbers that the number $w$ does not contain zero in the decimal record.

negatives.text:
Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n^2$ contains only the digit zero;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n$ contains only the digit zero.

Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n^2$ contains no digit zero;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)^2}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n$ contains no digit zero.

positive_text:
There are $W$ points on the circle. Andriy and Olesya play the game as follows. Andriy goes first. They take turns and connect two of the given points with a chord, if it does not cross any of the prior chords. The one after whose move there is a triangle formed by the drawn chords wins. Who will win the fair game if:

a) $W=14$;
b) $W=15$?

negatives.text:
Given $N$ points on a circle, Andriy and Olesya take turns drawing chords between any two points, avoiding intersections with previously drawn chords. The player who draws the last possible chord wins. Who wins if:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

On a circle with $N$ points, players alternately draw chords between any two points, avoiding intersections. The player who completes a square using the drawn chords wins. Who wins if:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

Let there be $N$ points on a circle. Andriy and Olesya take turns drawing chords between any two points, ensuring they do not intersect existing chords. The player who cannot move loses. Who wins if:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

